# Confirms the float32/float64 `.pow()` → `.powf()` method-call fix
# (`pow_method_float_width.br`) is scoped to float receivers only: an `int` receiver's
# `.pow()` must keep transpiling to a literal `.pow(...)` call unchanged — Rust's own
# `isize::pow(exp: u32)` already exists, so the pre-existing (unmodified) passthrough
# emission is already correct Rust for it, unlike `f32`/`f64`, which have no `.pow()` at
# all. Not registered in tests/run.rs — `boring run` (the interpreter) has no int `.pow()`
# case at all and errors outright (`no method 'pow' on Int`), a separate, pre-existing
# interpreter limitation this fix doesn't touch either way.
#
# The exponent argument is passed as a bare integer literal (`2`) rather than an
# `int`-typed variable — Rust's own integer-literal type inference resolves it to the
# `u32` `isize::pow` expects either way, so this stays a real, compiling, self-contained
# regression case without also exercising the separate, pre-existing `int`-typed-exponent
# widening gap (`isize` vs. `pow`'s required `u32`), which is unrelated to this fix.

int powIntParam(int base):
    base.pow(2)

print powIntParam(2)
